<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=ski">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ski</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 6300 BCE</span> · Also known as Skis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Skis are runners designed to glide over snow, used in pairs and fastened to the boots by bindings. Skis appeared before the wheel, with the oldest wooden skis found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> (c. 6300 to 5000 BCE), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> (c. 5200 BCE) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> (c. 3200 BCE). These early skis were not made for recreation or speed: their purpose was to keep the user on top of the snow when hunting or at war, and a walking stick helped with balance. The word comes from the Old Norse skíð. Nordic technology was adapted in the early 20th century to allow turns at speed, and ski lifts helped produce alpine skis.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ski.jpg" alt="Ski" width="330" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HopsonRoad · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cross-country_equipment--Skate_and_Classic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
